!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/38/io/ltr8/http/http.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/38/m/meta.tn"
@doc:"""
  HTTP's own vocabulary, for a schema that describes or carries HTTP: a method, a status code, a header field
  name, and a URI template.

  This project's library rather than the spec's, deliberately. These are HTTP's terms, not formats every
  schema needs, and exactly the names a converted API description declares for itself -- so they belong in a
  library a schema opts into by importing, where they reserve their names only in the schemas that asked for
  them. tson-java's bundled net.tn is the same shape for network identifiers; a URI template is not one of
  those (it builds a URI rather than naming a host), and its uses outside describing an API are few.

  It imports nothing, as net.tn does: every type is built from meta's own constructors, so a schema may import
  it beside core and a meta layer may import it beside meta.tn -- meta-http.tn does, for its operations --
  without one name reaching it twice.
  """
{
  @doc:"A request method (RFC 9110 §9), the ones an API description has a use for."
  http_method => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"A response status code (RFC 9110 §15): three digits, 100 to 599. Refined, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer_type { min: 100  max: 599 }

  @doc:"""
    A header field name (RFC 9110 §5.1): a token, compared case-insensitively. Held folded
    (NFKC_CASEFOLD, which over a token's ASCII characters is lowercasing), so `Content-Type` and
    `content-type` are one value.
    """
  header_name => !text_type { pattern: "[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+"  normalization: NFKC_CASEFOLD }

  @doc:"""
    A URI template (RFC 6570) at levels 1 and 2: literal text with `{var}` expressions, where `{+var}`
    (reserved expansion) leaves a value's `/`, `?` and `#` unencoded and `{#var}` adds a fragment. Level 2 is
    the line because `{+var}` is what an identity path needs -- `/{+schemaPath}` serves a path with slashes in
    it, where `/{schemaPath}` would percent-encode them -- and levels 3 and 4 (several variables to an
    expression, `{?q,page}` query forms, prefixes and explode) describe expansion rather than a route a
    server can match.

    A variable name is ASCII letters, digits and `_`, dot-separated; RFC 6570's percent-encoded name
    characters are not admitted. A pattern checks the shape. What it cannot check is the template against
    what declares its variables -- an operation's PATH parameters -- which TsonApiDescription checks when it
    reads a description.
    """
  uri_template => !text_type {
    pattern: "([^\\p{Cc} \"'%<>\\\\^`{|}]|%[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}|\\{[+#]?[A-Za-z0-9_]+([.][A-Za-z0-9_]+)*\\})*"
  }
}
